WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.810
FRANCES ANGULO: First
things first, I'm Frances.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.990
I'm the lead program manager for
Chromebook adoption at Google.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.470
And so today, we're going to
talk all things Chromebook.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.320
And I'm joined by Russ White,
who we'll talk about Grab

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.820
and Go today.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.092
If you've been using
the Dory app, great.

00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:24.050
If you haven't used it
yet, give it a download.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.030
That's how we'll take
questions for this session.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.818
So go and get that
started, post them there.

00:00:29.818 --> 00:00:32.360
We have a whole panel of people
here to answer your questions

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.800
about Chromebook deployments.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.170
So please go ahead
and get that queued

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.830
up so that you can put all
your burning questions there.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.260
What we're going to talk about
today is, why Chromebooks?

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.177
I think most of you are
here because you know.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.920
But I'm just going to make
sure we're aligned on that.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.075
We'll talk about driving
adoption in your organization.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:51.950
I'm going to tell you
all the secrets and how

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.060
we did it at Google.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.710
And then, of course,
Russ is going

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.130
to talk about Grab and Go.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.440
So the first thing I want
to do is just share with you

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.620
that Google actually is solving
a lot of the same problems

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.240
that most of our IT
customers are solving.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.500
We're a mixed-fleet ecosystem
of Mac, Windows, Linux,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.900
and Chromebook devices.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.800
We have a huge Chromebook fleet.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.300
As you can imagine, we're one
of Chrome Enterprise's largest

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.260
customers.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.260
And we also care about
improving operational efficiency

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.940
and reducing IT spent.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.060
And so that's one of the reasons
in a long list of reasons

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.800
why we love Chromebooks.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.840
So from a user
standpoint, Chromebooks

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.000
offer a distraction-free
productivity environment

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.420
that's purpose-built
for Cloud workflows.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.580
And obviously, from a
business standpoint,

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.940
they're security
built-in by design,

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.210
an intelligent investment,
both in management and cost.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.730
And so because of that
whole slew of reasons,

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.790
Chromebooks are a great
choice for our organization

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.350
and hopefully, yours.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.770
And so with all of that in mind,
Google set out with this goal

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.990
that users are
happy and productive

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.640
on Chromebook devices.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.100
And that's a very
specifically worded goal.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.960
We could have set a goal that
said, users use Chromebooks.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.820
And that just means we give
Chromebooks to everybody.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:12.360
But we have this
secondary achievement

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.860
of making sure that people are
happy, and can do their jobs,

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.930
and can do them well, and
are excited about being

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.216
on the platform.

00:02:18.216 --> 00:02:22.000
And so if you share those goals,
then this session, I think,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.010
is going to be particularly
interesting for you.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.510
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.630
to walk you through what we call
this adoption timeline, which

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.600
is from start to finish,
from evaluating Chromebooks

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.030
as a potential device to
put into your fleet, all

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.700
the way to getting users up
and running on that device,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.920
and how to handle
changes, including

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.700
attrition from the platform.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.400
And what you'll
see at the end is,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.942
we're going to
provide some artifacts

00:02:43.942 --> 00:02:46.210
for each stage of this
journey so that wherever

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.970
you are in this timeline,
we've offered something that

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.430
will help you either in the
challenges of that phase

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.400
or maybe even move on
further down the timeline

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.430
than you're in now.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.730
So from the onset,
you're probably

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.190
going to be evaluating
what types of hardware

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.550
are sufficient or acceptable
for your environment.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.050
And one of the
questions that we get

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.842
asked, actually, quite
a lot is, how do you

00:03:08.842 --> 00:03:12.180
decide what platforms to
bring in to the Google space?

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.680
What makes Chromebooks great?

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.140
How do you determine what are
the qualities of a trusted

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.052
platform?

00:03:17.052 --> 00:03:18.510
And what are the
ways that you look

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.400
at security and management?

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.740
And so there's an artifact,
if you haven't seen it,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.290
that we've recently published
that talks about exactly

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.780
what Google expects from a
platform when they bring it

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.740
into the fleet, including
prevention in terms of risk

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:36.720
of infiltration into the
platform, risk detection,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.610
the ability to
manage the device,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.337
how much control we have over
what kind of data it consumes.

00:03:41.337 --> 00:03:43.420
All of those features are
listed in this document.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.000
And again, at the end,
I'm going to give you

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.917
this list of artifacts
that you can reference.

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:48.877
But when we evaluate
all of our platforms,

00:03:48.877 --> 00:03:50.460
those are the things
that we consider.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.730
And Chrome certainly passes the
test, especially with the way

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.045
that the security is
designed in the platform.

00:03:56.045 --> 00:03:58.170
And so if you're on board
with bringing Chromebooks

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.870
into your environment, the
second most common question

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.590
we get is, which Chromebooks
should we bring in?

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.400
There is, of course,
Google-made hardware.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.100
And we're really excited about
it, and we think it's great.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.570
But we're also including Google
support third-party vendor

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:13.110
hardware.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.704
So there's a lot of
different choices.

00:04:14.704 --> 00:04:16.079
And when you're
looking at what's

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:18.540
the best device to bring in,
of course, the first things

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.450
that you're going to think
about are performance and specs,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.670
and what types of work
that people are doing.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.550
But when we surveyed Googlers
as they're bringing Chromebooks

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.977
into the ecosystem
of choice, the things

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:30.810
that were most important
to them-- and these

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.750
are in order-- are they
wanted a lightweight device.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.068
So our workforce is
generally quite mobile.

00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:38.610
A lot of people move
between offices.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.990
They do quite a
lot of traveling.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.042
We exist in cities where there's
a lot of heavy commuting.

00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:45.000
And so people are carrying
their laptops a lot.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.578
And so weight was one of
the biggest requirements.

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:49.620
The Pixelbook is the
lightest device in the fleet

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.010
that we offer.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.860
And so it was obviously
very satisfactory

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.750
to the users who adopted it.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.570
They also want high-quality
hardware look and feel.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.190
People who are
doing important work

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.470
want to feel like the hardware
that they have supports

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.660
that important work,
especially when you

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.750
have a choice between other
premium hardware options.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.490
They want a device that's
supported by their peers.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.665
There is this phenomenon.

00:05:10.665 --> 00:05:13.290
If you choose a Chromebook, and
you're really excited about it,

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:15.790
and you enter a team where no
one else is using that device,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:19.090
that it can cause people to take
a second look at their decision

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.250
and really question if
that was a good choice.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.270
And so having peers understand
what the device is capable of

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.580
and making sure that it's
pervasive across roles

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:27.560
is really important.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.310
They want a great
battery life, of course.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:34.170
And a touch screen was actually
close to the top four reasons

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.440
that we got from Googlers.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.612
And so achieving
all of these things

00:05:37.612 --> 00:05:39.570
was actually quite simple
for us with Pixelbook

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.400
because it is
high-performance, it

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.810
is a high-quality
piece of hardware.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.930
It has a touch screen, it
can work in tablet mode,

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.530
and it's the lightest
device in the fleet.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.830
And so because of that, the
employees that we survey,

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.710
90% of them are quite
satisfied and want to keep

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.500
and would recommend their
Pixelbook to another team

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.902
member.

00:05:56.902 --> 00:05:58.860
And so once you've selected
the kind of devices

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.818
that you're going to
bring in your environment,

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:02.820
now the next question
is more strategic.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.220
Are we going to mandate,
from a business standpoint,

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.400
that users will now
adopt Chromebooks

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.600
that's our new fleet?

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.688
Or is there going to be
an ecosystem of choice

00:06:11.688 --> 00:06:13.230
where people can
choose, and you need

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.400
to influence their behavior?

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.400
And so at Google,
we actually do have

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:20.342
both scenarios and different
teams for different reasons.

00:06:20.342 --> 00:06:22.050
And so each of those
different approaches

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:25.140
requires different strategic
ways of executing on them.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.810
Managing change
and telling people

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.060
that their platform
preference is not

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.850
going to be part of the story
when they select their device

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.065
requires things like
making sure that you're

00:06:35.065 --> 00:06:36.690
choosing the right
people to start with

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.240
and expanding your scope
to better candidates.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.070
And if you saw our
presentation last year--

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.080
and it's up on the channel,
and you can reference it if you

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:43.680
didn't--

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.410
we talked a lot
about segmentation

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.480
and how we think about
segmenting user types

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.520
and identifying good
candidates for Chrome OS

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.220
before we push
the device in mass

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.270
to people who might
not be prepared for it.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:57.330
And what you'll learn
later in this presentation

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.750
is that the percentage of
people who don't have experience

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.820
with Chrome OS is actually
a lot higher than even

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.532
we anticipated within Google.

00:07:04.532 --> 00:07:06.990
And so that means that if you're
going to push this change,

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.770
it requires a lot of care.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.980
And if you're working
in an ecosystem

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.345
where people have a choice,
that's a different story.

00:07:13.345 --> 00:07:15.720
And so that's where we do a
lot of marketing initiatives.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.220
What we've found is
that getting the device

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.860
in the hands of the users is
one of the most effective ways

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.893
to get them excited
about the device.

00:07:21.893 --> 00:07:23.310
And so Russ is
going to talk later

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.560
about Grab and Go, which is
one of the key initiatives

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.990
that help users get
experience with Chrome OS

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:31.780
before they make the plunge
or are exposed to the change.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.708
And so I call this
out because if you're

00:07:33.708 --> 00:07:35.250
going to deploy
Chromebooks, you have

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.740
to consider which of
these, if not both,

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.690
are going to be true for
you and then consider

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:42.180
that either change management
or a lot of education

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.850
and marketing is going
to be required depending

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.110
on what you do.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.860
Obviously, from a
provisioning standpoint,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.235
we're really excited that
Chrome is so easy to provision.

00:07:50.235 --> 00:07:52.080
So in the case of
a Chromebook, it's

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.660
really a matter of enterprise
enrolling the device

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.403
and delivering it to the desk.

00:07:56.403 --> 00:07:58.320
Because of the nature
of the way that we image

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.810
alternative platforms
like Mac and Windows,

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.180
we save 109 minutes
every time a Chromebook

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.760
is deployed in place of one
of those other platforms.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.585
And so from a management
and operation standpoint,

00:08:08.585 --> 00:08:09.960
the more Chromebook
we deploy, we

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.330
have this sort of
exponential gain.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.070
And that's just
based on the nature

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:18.702
of images for those other
platforms within our ecosystem.

00:08:18.702 --> 00:08:20.910
And so you've provisioned
the device, which is great.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.260
And you've delivered
it to the users desk.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.920
And this is where we
actually learned quite a lot.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.810
And this whole
presentation is designed

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.053
to try to prevent
you from having

00:08:30.053 --> 00:08:31.470
to learn all of
the things that we

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.360
spent a long time figuring out.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.090
And so what we first tried
to do was just respect

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.620
that people were
new to the platform

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.770
and then give them as much
information as possible

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.750
for them to be successful.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.640
And the way that we
determine success

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.220
at this point, which is getting
up and running, and keeping

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.410
the Chromebook as
your primary device,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.120
is, do they stay
on the platform?

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.750
And so if we're experiencing
attrition from the platform,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.060
that means we're
doing something wrong.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.402
And so what we learned is
there's three basic phases

00:08:59.402 --> 00:09:01.110
that users are going
through when they're

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.040
adopting this new platform.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.770
The first and foremost
thing on their mind,

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.040
especially with new
hires, is, I just

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.878
want to get email, calendar, and
the essential work apps set up.

00:09:11.878 --> 00:09:13.420
That's the first
thing on their mind.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:16.620
So when we talk about
sophisticated, cool pro tips,

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.710
and productivity hacks,
and workflow examples,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:20.850
it's way too much.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.470
It actually underlines
that they're

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.550
about to experience
a learning curve.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:27.060
And so we don't present that
mountain on the first day

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.470
that somebody gets a Chromebook.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.960
We sort of ease them into
the basic critical things

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:31.900
that they need.

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:33.982
The second phase after
the first few days

00:09:33.982 --> 00:09:35.940
and once you get into
that first or second week

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:37.770
is personalization.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.945
So people tend to
download personal apps.

00:09:39.945 --> 00:09:41.820
They're starting to
translate their workflows

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.050
from other platforms.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:46.312
And so that's basic information
of tips and tricks--

00:09:46.312 --> 00:09:47.520
I used to do this on Windows.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.660
How do I do it on Chrome?

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.160
Or I'm more familiar
with Mac, kind

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.230
of curated information
that will help people

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:55.830
ease into the transition is what
we present at that time frame.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.230
And then once you get a few
weeks further down the line,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.060
that's when we get
into the productivity

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.700
hacks-- here's how you use
hotkeys and keyboard shortcuts,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.120
and those types of things.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.940
And so this is an
important lesson for us

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:09.510
because 84% of the Googlers
that were assigned a Chromebook

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:13.800
device this year had little
to zero use of the platform.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:14.820
And that's a lot.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.780
That's self-reported when
we survey the new people who

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.040
were assigned this year.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:21.900
And so that really
taught us a lot

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.630
about the type of information
that we need to provide

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.260
and how we need
to break it down.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.090
And so again, the artifacts that
we use and the strategy will

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.520
be given to you guys at the
end of this presentation,

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.020
including tips and
tricks campaigns,

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.920
and the type of content we
have there, and also service.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.332
So we do survey all
new users of Chrome OS,

00:10:41.332 --> 00:10:43.290
and we ask them about
their initial impressions

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.540
so that we can give direction
for additional resources

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:46.435
that they need.

00:10:46.435 --> 00:10:48.810
But another important part of
running Chromebook adoption

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.670
is staying on top of
a monitoring goal.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.540
So if you set a target
for Chromebook adoption,

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.880
or you're just trying to
understand the basic health

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:58.710
when you introduce this
in the environment,

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.650
there's a massive monitoring
and dashboard need.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:04.320
And we've spent
so many iterations

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.820
designing charts
and figuring out

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:08.010
what questions to ask--
should this be a part chart?

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.223
Should this be overlaid
on something else?

00:11:10.223 --> 00:11:11.640
And so we've done
all of that work

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.250
and provided a template
example for you

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.160
on what a dashboard could look
like if you're trying to answer

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.420
some of these questions.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.545
Because these are the five
things that we generally

00:11:20.545 --> 00:11:21.640
wanted to answer.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.370
How many people
have a Chromebook

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:25.110
versus how many
people are using it

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.580
as their actual daily driver?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.520
So there is a circumstance where
an employee could potentially

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:30.930
have more than one device.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.040
And we treat that
circumstance differently.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.520
So if they have a Chromebook
and also another platform,

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.290
that's not the same
definition of success

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.943
as if a Chromebook is their
only operating system.

00:11:39.943 --> 00:11:41.610
And so that's question
number one for us

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.710
in terms of defining adoption.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.345
We also want to know why
people leave Chrome OS.

00:11:46.345 --> 00:11:47.970
And you could just
ask them, and you'll

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.220
get this massive
flow of open text

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:51.750
that you have to
later interpret.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.050
We've also done that for you.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:54.960
So we've given you
a list of reasons

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.778
that you can use to aggregate
and collect that data.

00:11:57.778 --> 00:11:59.820
How many people are choosing
a laptop at the time

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.278
that they have a
hardware decision?

00:12:01.278 --> 00:12:02.790
So any milestone
where people could

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.890
select an alternative
platform, whether it's

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.900
moving to a new role
or refreshing a device,

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.790
we want to know what
the trends are there.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.860
New hire assignment
is absolutely critical

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:13.680
because it sets the tone for
what the future will look

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.210
like in terms of
growth if you're

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:18.180
trying to create a new
population of users

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.720
versus focusing on converting
the current population.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.880
And then lastly, where
does Chrome assignment

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.840
stand against some of
the other platforms?

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.860
So our goal is that
Chrome assignments

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:31.590
are above the other platforms
and, obviously, in the up

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.180
and to the right line.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.510
So we've created some
examples on those questions

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.890
and how we track
and monitor them

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.720
so that you can
potentially take that back.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.120
And so I mentioned that
we survey new users.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.990
But we also surveyed this
super important and very

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.090
well-respected group
at Google, which are

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.860
people who leave the platform.

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:53.850
And so they tell one of the
most important data parts

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:54.780
of the story.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.615
What we've found is that
you could ask a user, hey,

00:12:57.615 --> 00:12:59.990
if we gave you a Chromebook,
would you be able to use it?

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.390
And you'll get a lot of
anecdotal and hypothetical

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:04.310
reasons why they
could or could not.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:06.620
But actually asking
someone to try it

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.710
and figuring out when they leave
the platform in their journey

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:13.500
why they're leaving produces the
most valuable insights for us.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.430
And so what we learned is
that 30% of the people who

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.110
left the platform
left for reasons

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:21.380
that we could in theory control,
and so reason categories like

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.380
education, resources,
training, materials,

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:27.600
communication about what the
platform is and what it does.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.600
And so because of that,
that attrition data

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:33.440
both tells us how to set the
direction to maintain retention

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.290
but also gives us the
most actionable feedback

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:37.730
for product teams.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:40.850
And we'll talk a little
bit about that right now.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:45.140
So our goal is, obviously, that
people can use the platform

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.590
as much as possible.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:48.350
But we also have
this responsibility

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:51.500
to communicate this story
of, if people can't use it

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.960
or don't want to,
let's figure out why

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.510
and make Chrome competitive
against the other platforms.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:01.280
And so we encourage all of
our customers to do the same.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.870
Filing a Chrome bug is step
one to collecting feedback.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:06.840
And Google does this as well.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.770
And then stack ranking and
prioritizing your requests

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:11.120
is the next phase.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.857
And so we presented a
template for you in our kit

00:14:13.857 --> 00:14:15.440
that you'll get at
the end of the day.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:17.273
And these might seem
like obvious questions.

00:14:17.273 --> 00:14:19.880
But this helps
narrow exactly what's

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:22.970
required for you to both collect
and monitor your own story.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:26.210
But it also creates an artifact
that your technical account

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.770
manager or customer
success manager

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:31.230
can help navigate and
escalate where necessary.

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:35.570
And so this is driven by that
attrition feedback and also

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:37.460
the new user feedback.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:39.620
Creating those surveys
in a way that's

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:42.350
easy to monitor, and measure,
and present up on a dashboard

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:43.020
can be hard.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:44.990
It requires a lot
of data aggregation.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.810
And we dedicated a huge amount
of UX research resources

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.960
to this so that we can present
these artifacts in a way

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:54.810
that we think are intelligent
and are actually valid research

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:55.310
methods.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.740
So you'll get those too.

00:14:57.740 --> 00:15:01.790
And so just to summarize where
we sit in terms of Chromebook

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:05.870
at Google, every time we
deploy a Chromebook based

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:08.180
on the devices that
we're purchasing,

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:11.270
we save 50% just
in device costs.

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:12.650
Every time we
deploy a Chromebook

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.317
over another platform,
which I mentioned

00:15:14.317 --> 00:15:16.070
before, we save 109 minutes.

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:19.080
And for every single Chrome
OS support interaction,

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.710
there are three from
another platform at Google.

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:23.960
And so that means those other
platforms are generating

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:25.127
three times as much support.

00:15:25.127 --> 00:15:26.892
And there's a lot
of reasons for that,

00:15:26.892 --> 00:15:28.100
and we could talk about them.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.080
But obviously, they have
to do with complexity

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.210
of the platform, and the
way that the images work,

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:35.210
and a number of other things
that users can experience just

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:38.780
based on what the
platform is capable of.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:42.050
And we do have approaching
90,000 active Chrome devices

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:42.890
at Google.

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:46.310
And I mentioned before that 90%
of our users who have Pixelbook

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.650
are satisfied with the devices
and would recommend them

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:50.035
to their employees.

00:15:50.035 --> 00:15:52.160
And so based on this timeline
and all of the things

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:54.410
that we've accomplished, we
see this as great success.

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:57.500
We tripled our adoption
population since last year

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.920
with adoption being defined
as users using the platform

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.120
as their primary device.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.500
And so this URL is available.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:09.970
You guys can also get access
to this after the session.

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.370
But what this URL points
to is the timeline

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.340
that we spoke about and all of
the artifacts that are related

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:16.750
to each of those milestones.

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.630
So that includes surveys,
tips and tricks campaigns,

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:23.500
the dashboard template example,
and any other resources

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.810
that we felt could
be beneficial to you

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.600
wherever you are in the stage
of your adoption journey.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.370
So definitely take
a snapshot of that

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.820
so you can download
it and have a look.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:37.430
And so I mentioned before
that one of the best ways

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.080
to get users excited
about the platform

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.030
is to get the device in
the hands of the user.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:43.460
And Grab and Go has been one
of our most successful ways

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:44.100
to do that.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.560
So Russ is here to
talk about that.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.310
RUSS WHITE: Great.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:48.320
Thanks, Frances.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:48.900
I'm Russ.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:50.450
I'm going to talk to you a
little bit about Grab and Go,

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:52.130
our self-service
Chromebook loaner

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.775
program that we run at Google.

00:16:53.775 --> 00:16:56.150
We're going to talk a little
bit about why we created it,

00:16:56.150 --> 00:17:00.260
why we're still working on
it, how it works, what it is,

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:02.510
and then talk a little bit
about some of the successes

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:05.270
that we've seen at Google,
and, perhaps, how you can

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:06.440
make some of those your own.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:07.550
We have some really
great options

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:09.592
for how you might be able
to deploy your own Grab

00:17:09.592 --> 00:17:11.079
and Go program.

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:13.890
So first up, let's
talk about why.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.599
Google is just like
any other company.

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:19.349
We lose a lot of
time to IT downtime.

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:21.300
Our devices break just
like everyone else's.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:22.925
And when we first
started this program,

00:17:22.925 --> 00:17:25.510
we were focused on
minimizing that downtime.

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:29.160
So IHS Markit estimates that
North American businesses

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:34.690
lose over $700 billion a
year just to IT downtime.

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:39.480
And we also knew that the
traditional loaner program

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.210
journey really isn't
great for anybody.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:44.233
So our users had to
come to help desk

00:17:44.233 --> 00:17:45.400
when the help desk was open.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:46.060
They had to wait in line.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:47.940
They had to get a
loaner device that

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.053
probably wasn't a great device.

00:17:50.053 --> 00:17:52.470
And they had to do that again
when they were returning it.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:55.770
For IT staff, they were
maintaining those devices,

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.390
ordering them,
provisioning them.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:59.290
And they also had to spend
all the time handing them out.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:01.332
And none of that is really
fun work for our team.

00:18:01.332 --> 00:18:03.240
So we had a lot of
signals telling us,

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.440
go in this direction.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:07.020
We also had a couple
of opportunities

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.530
that we knew about--

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:13.230
the fact that Google
employees use our help desks

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:15.960
at least once per year on
average, and oftentimes,

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:17.890
quite a bit more than that.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:21.090
And we also knew that around
80% or 85% of Googlers

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:23.670
could be fully
productive on Chrome OS.

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.310
Not all of them required
a Windows or a Mac OS

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:27.070
to be productive.

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:29.237
So we knew that we could
put a device in their hands

00:18:29.237 --> 00:18:30.900
that they could get
their work done on.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:33.160
And then why we're continuing
to work on this program?

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:36.360
We're still iterating on it,
we're still making it better.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:41.725
Around 35% of Googlers used the
Grab and Go program in 2018.

00:18:41.725 --> 00:18:42.600
Around half of them--

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.820
18%-- were using the
program to work around

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.340
a true productivity blocking
problem, so some IT problem.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.290
Maybe their device was
broken, or forgotten,

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:50.708
or something like that.

00:18:50.708 --> 00:18:52.500
And then the other
half, another 18% or so,

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:55.233
were using the program for
more convenience reasons,

00:18:55.233 --> 00:18:57.150
maybe be picking up a
device between meetings,

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.817
or maybe they're
traveling and just want

00:18:58.817 --> 00:19:02.530
to pick up a laptop when they
arrive, something like that.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:05.610
So at its simplest,
this is Grab and Go.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:07.190
You walk up to one
of our shelves.

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:08.190
They are just out there.

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.823
You can use it
whenever you'd like.

00:19:09.823 --> 00:19:12.240
You walk up, pick up a device,
sign it in with your Google

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:13.920
account, and get back to work.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.920
When you're done, you
bring it back, plug it in,

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:16.630
and that's it.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:18.553
There's no IT team
intervention required.

00:19:18.553 --> 00:19:19.970
You don't have to
talk to anybody.

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:23.000
You just walk up
and take a laptop.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.540
So the core components
of the program

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.490
start with the physical domain.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.887
So the devices are
a key part of this.

00:19:31.887 --> 00:19:34.220
We make sure that our devices
that we put in our program

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:35.450
are Enterprise grade.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.040
We want Googlers
to be productive

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:38.390
when they're using the devices.

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:40.220
We want them to be durable.

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:43.190
They need to survive going
from person to person,

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.012
being thrown back on the shelf,
being thrown in your bag.

00:19:46.012 --> 00:19:47.720
And we also want it
to be cost-conscious.

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:49.520
We don't want to spend so
much money that we're really

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:50.720
giving everyone two devices.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.280
We want it to be, perhaps, a
little bit cheaper than that.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.170
So that's the first piece.

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.510
The second is the
shelves that we buy.

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:00.230
So we prefer to
have shelves that

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:02.570
are modular because we have
offices of different sizes.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:04.070
Sometimes we need
a little tiny one,

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:06.800
sometimes we need
a very big one.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:09.200
In all of these cases, we
want charging to be built-in.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:10.700
That's a key part
of the experience.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:13.283
You need to be able to pick up
a device and walk away with it,

00:20:13.283 --> 00:20:14.690
and know that it's
fully charged.

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:16.857
So that needs to be an
obvious feature of the shelf.

00:20:16.857 --> 00:20:19.022
And we also want them to
have key safety features.

00:20:19.022 --> 00:20:21.230
And that's something that
we didn't think about a lot

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:24.950
early on, but we
really do a lot now.

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:27.110
We want them to be
resistant to tipping.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:28.377
We want them to be grounded.

00:20:28.377 --> 00:20:30.710
We want them to be coated
with a non-conductive material

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:34.510
so that you can remove that
from your line of thinking.

00:20:34.510 --> 00:20:37.460
And the last piece is, we've
designed our signs and stickers

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.870
to be very self-explanatory.

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:42.920
We found that the loaner
flow that we designed

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.750
was a little bit
awkward for some users.

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:47.000
It felt like you were walking
up and stealing a laptop

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:48.600
if it wasn't explained at all.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.720
So the signs that
we put up there

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.162
are very clear, very concise.

00:20:52.162 --> 00:20:53.120
They are stepped signs.

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:55.250
It's actually very similar to
what you saw in the last sign

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:57.833
saying, no, just really pick it
up, sign it, and bring it back

00:20:57.833 --> 00:20:58.980
when you're done.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.890
And we also placed
instructions at key points

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:01.723
in the user journey.

00:21:01.723 --> 00:21:04.100
So when you open
the laptop, there

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:07.040
are stickers inside the laptop
that explain how to sign it,

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:11.840
how to connect to Wi-Fi, how
to return it, things like that.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.640
Another key ingredient of our
program is Chrome OS itself.

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:19.770
Chrome OS makes it very easy
to just sign it and get back

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:20.270
to work.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.800
So things like Chrome Sync or
the integrations with G Suite

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.500
products make it
incredibly easy to get back

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:28.390
to work once you've signed in.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:30.850
These devices are
very, very secure

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:33.070
because of the built-in
features of Chrome OS.

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:35.680
Things like verified
boot, automatic updates,

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:38.180
signing restriction, those are
all super easy to configure.

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:39.805
We'll talk a little
bit more about that

00:21:39.805 --> 00:21:41.500
in the next couple of slides.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:44.620
And these devices are very,
very easy to maintain.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.620
We manage our entire fleet just
with the click of a few buttons

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.050
and the Admin API,
the admin panel.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:52.660
So we have a very easy
time managing a fleet

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:57.760
of upwards of 2000 devices.

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:01.560
So it's not all just signs,
and devices, and Chrome OS.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:04.590
There is, actually, quite a
large management infrastructure

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:07.453
that we've built. At
the center of all of it

00:22:07.453 --> 00:22:09.120
is an App Engine
application that's kind

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.250
of the brain of the program.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:15.330
That application is where
program administrators monitor

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.310
the performance of
the program, who's

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.673
assigned to what, which
shelves might be low on stock,

00:22:19.673 --> 00:22:20.590
those kinds of things.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:22.965
And it also is streaming all
of that data and information

00:22:22.965 --> 00:22:24.210
to BigQuery.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.310
Another key piece of it
is Google's Admin API.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:28.268
That's where we enforce
a lot of those policies

00:22:28.268 --> 00:22:30.810
like verified boot.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:32.610
One other key policy
that we enforce

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:35.670
there is, a custom-built
Chrome application

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:38.770
is pushed to every single
Grab and Go device.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:40.920
So when the user signs in,
the first thing they see

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:43.420
is that application popping up
and greeting them and saying,

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:44.910
hey, welcome to Grab and Go.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:46.980
Here's how you use this device.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.230
That application
also guides them

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.670
through setting
your return dates.

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:52.087
They can ask
questions there, they

00:22:52.087 --> 00:22:54.373
can report problems,
those kinds of things.

00:22:54.373 --> 00:22:56.040
And then, of course,
all of these things

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:59.367
allow for integration with
the other internal tools.

00:22:59.367 --> 00:23:01.950
So we have a ticketing system,
we have an inventory management

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:05.410
system, those kinds of things.

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:08.500
So like I said, when we
first deployed Grab and Go,

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:13.020
we were doing so to avoid
lost productivity situations,

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.250
so forgotten, broken laptops.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.050
But after we deployed
it, we've found

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:19.090
that Googlers were using
this program for things

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.160
that we didn't really expect.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:22.660
And we're still not
exactly sure how

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:26.560
to measure the benefit
of those things.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:28.810
So some of those
things are travel.

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:30.520
So we've found that
a lot of Googlers

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:33.570
will actually leave
their laptop at home,

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:36.310
travel to another office, pick
up a laptop there, use that,

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:38.018
put it back on the
shelf, and then travel

00:23:38.018 --> 00:23:40.460
back home just to avoid
carrying a laptop with them.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:44.800
We also have big groups of
shift workers inside of Google.

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:49.030
We recently published a blog on
how Waymo is using Grab and Go.

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:52.537
So they have shift workers
testing driverless cars.

00:23:52.537 --> 00:23:54.370
And they take a Grab
and Go device with them

00:23:54.370 --> 00:23:55.090
every time they go out.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.270
So there's a couple of
shifts of those workers.

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:58.900
And they've saved
money on not needing

00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:00.232
to buy devices for all of them.

00:24:00.232 --> 00:24:02.440
And they also have a really
great management solution

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:03.390
with Grab and Go.

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.650
And then, of course,
like Frances was saying,

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:08.800
it's a really great way to get
Chromebooks in people's hands.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.870
Using the device is
a really great way

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:13.510
to break down barriers
and understand the power

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:15.297
and ease of use of Chrome OS.

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:22.110
So we're very happy
with Grab and Go Google.

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:24.870
We've found that each
loan really only needs

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:27.360
to save about 25 minutes
of productivity time

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.010
to pay for itself.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:31.890
And our most conservative
estimates show that in 2018, we

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:34.950
saved around $17
million, if you convert

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:36.210
productivity time to dollars.

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:38.040
So this is definitely
something we're

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:39.510
going to continue investing in.

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.458
And it's something that, like
I said, we're very happy with.

00:24:42.458 --> 00:24:44.750
If you're thinking about
deploying your own Grab and Go

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:47.150
program, but you're seeing
all of these pieces that

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:49.070
need to be put in
place and thinking,

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:51.122
I will not do that,
I have good news.

00:24:51.122 --> 00:24:53.330
We have really great partners
that can work with you.

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:55.610
So we've spent the
last year or so

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:58.250
working with some of
Google's closest partners.

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:02.030
The list is much longer
than just what you see here.

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:05.035
Their services range anywhere
from procuring the hardware,

00:25:05.035 --> 00:25:07.160
setting up the devices,
customizing the application

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.520
that we built, doing
inventory management,

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:13.520
training of your team on how to
run the program, anything up to

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:16.590
and including even
white glove service.

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:19.450
If you're more of a DIYer
and you're thinking,

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:22.080
this looks great, I can
do that, that's excellent.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:25.448
We have code all
open-sourced on GitHub--

00:25:25.448 --> 00:25:26.490
github.com/google/loaner.

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:29.432
Everything's there from code
to art, to deployment plans,

00:25:29.432 --> 00:25:31.890
to whatever-- everything you'd
need to deploy your own Grab

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:32.895
and Go program.

00:25:32.895 --> 00:25:35.478
And like I said, the partners
talk to your partner.

00:25:35.478 --> 00:25:37.020
Definitely start a
conversation there

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:38.760
or feel free to
engage with the ones

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:40.860
that we showed on
the last slide.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:44.530
Well, thank you, everyone, for
coming and thank you again.

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:45.330
Have a great day.

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:47.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:49.580
[MUSIC PLAYING]

